    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to providing for financing by the dormitory authority of construction and equipping of primary care health facilities by the Primary Care Development Corporation",
    "basePrintNo": "S5645",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5645-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1676 & 1680, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5645\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the public authorities law, in relation to providing for\nfinancing by the dormitory authority of construction and equipping of\nprimary care health facilities by the Primary Care Development\nCorporation\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo enable the Primary Care Development Corporation to apply for\nfunding through the Dormitory Authority of the State of New York for\nthe financing of capital improvements for the primary healthcare\nfacilities of the State to meet the primary care expansion goals of\nfederal and State healthcare reform initiatives in underserved\ncommunities throughout New York.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 amends paragraph (b) of subdivision 2 of section 1676 of the\npublic authorities law by adding a new undesignated paragraph to\nallow the Primary Care Development Corporation to finance capital\nimprovements for primary care health facilities through the Dormitory\nAuthority.\n\nSection 2. Amends subdivision 1 of section 1680 of the public\nauthorities law by adding a new undesignated paragraph to allow the\nPrimary Care Development Corporation to finance capital improvements\nfor primary care health facilities through the Dormitory Authority.\n\nSection 3. Provides any contracts entered into by the Dormitory\nAuthority of the State of New York pursuant to this act shall be\ndeemed to be within the meaning of such terms as set forth in article\n15-a of the executive law for purposes of promoting minority and\nwomen-owned business enterprises.\n\nSection 4. Provides an immediate effective date\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nSignificant recent changes in the healthcare environment are expected\nto generate an expansion in the demand for primary care. The federal\nAffordable Care Act (ACA) calls for major expansion and\ntransformation of primary care over the next four years to meet the\nexpected demand resulting from the enrollment of 32 million\nadditional individuals in health plans.\n\nThe Affordable Care Act contains numerous provisions that invest in\nprimary care capacity, including federal funding to expand\nfederally-qualified health centers (FQHCs). Expanding and\ntransforming primary care is a priority for the Administration and\nthe Legislature in order to reduce health care costs and improve\nhealth outcomes, particularly among Medicaid and public health\ninsurance enrollees.\n\nYet with limited capital funding available, New York State faces the\nchallenge of preserving existing primary care capacity and expanding\ncapacity to meet the growing demand, particularly in safety-net\n\n\ncommunities where healthcare facilities are in danger of closure.\nMedicaid Redesign Team Proposal 67 (adopted) calls for the use of\nHEAL capital funds and exploration of other capital/debt assistance\nto facilitate the restructuring of critical safety net hospitals,\nnursing homes and diagnostic and treatment centers (D&TCs). It is in\nNew York's interest to preserve and expand primary care during\nfacility restructurings or closures.\n\nThis bill would allow the Primary Care Development Corporation to use\ntax-exempt bond financing through the Dormitory Authority to\naccommodate these and other urgent critical needs related to\nexpanding primary care capacity.\n\nThe Primary Care Development Corporation (PCDC) is a non-profit\norganization whose mission is to expand and improve primary care in\nunderserved communities by providing capital access for acquisition,\nconstruction, improvements and equipment for primary healthcare\nproviders in New York State. PCDC has facilitated the investment of\n$260 million in over 91 primary care capital projects which have\nadded 630,000 square feet of space, created 2,300 permanent jobs and\nadded capacity to serve 575,000 additional primary care patients.\nPCDC has never had a loan default.\n\nPCDC is a national leader in primary care capital financing. The\ncapital access program has received over $8 million in Community\nDevelopment Financial Institutions (CDFI) grants and a $40 million\nallocation of New Markets Tax Credit for primary care expansion by\nthe U.S. Department of the Treasury. PCDC has administered a $10\nmillion statewide community health center capital grant program for\nthe New York State Department of Health and serves as the\nadministrator for underwriting and portfolio management services to\nthe Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA) federal loan\nguarantee program.\n\nPCDC has provided $65 million of bond financing for New York City\nprimary care providers through the Dormitory Authority under the\nState's Municipal Health Facility Improvement Program. This bill\nwould allow PCDC to provide tax-exempt financing for primary care\nproviders in both urban and rural underserved communities throughout\nNew York State.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate.\n\n",
